<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sangam literature</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 300 BCE–300 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sangam literature is the earliest body of Tamil poetry, composed roughly between 300 BCE and 300 CE and collected in the Eight Anthologies and the Ten Idylls. Its poems deal with love and with war, kings and patronage, and they are a main source for the early Chera, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> kingdoms. Tradition links it to literary academies, or sangams, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sangam-literature.jpg" alt="Sangam literature" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wikipedia Loves Art participant &quot;team_a&quot; · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WLA_lacma_12th_century_Maharishi_Agastya.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
